1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,900
A recall lets voters remove an elected official before the term ends.

2
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:09,680
Proposition five changes what happens next. It does not eliminate the power

3
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:15,140
to recall state officials. Today, a state recall ballot normally asks two

4
00:00:15,140 --> 00:00:19,860
questions: whether to remove the official, and who should replace them. If

5
00:00:19,860 --> 00:00:24,860
removal wins, the replacement candidate with the most votes takes office.

6
00:00:24,860 --> 00:00:29,220
Proposition five removes the replacement question. The office would be

7
00:00:29,220 --> 00:00:34,280
filled through succession, appointment, or a separate election, depending on

8
00:00:34,280 --> 00:00:36,280
the office and when the recall happens.

9
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:41,760
Imagine opening a ballot during a recall election. In a hypothetical crowded

10
00:00:41,760 --> 00:00:46,420
replacement contest, one candidate could lead with thirty votes out of a

11
00:00:46,420 --> 00:00:51,180
hundred. Winning the most votes is different from winning a majority. That

12
00:00:51,180 --> 00:00:55,820
is one concern motivating the change. Election staff would explain a

13
00:00:55,820 --> 00:00:57,280
different replacement process.

14
00:00:57,867 --> 00:01:02,287
If a legislator is recalled, a special election would generally fill the

15
00:01:02,287 --> 00:01:06,867
seat. Vacancies in most other state offices would be filled by gubernatorial

16
00:01:06,867 --> 00:01:12,107
appointment. So a separate replacement vote is not guaranteed for every

17
00:01:12,107 --> 00:01:16,847
office. For the governor, the lieutenant governor would take over. If the

18
00:01:16,847 --> 00:01:21,167
recall happens early enough, before the specified nomination deadline in the

19
00:01:21,167 --> 00:01:26,367
first two years, voters elect a new governor at a later statewide election.

20
00:01:26,367 --> 00:01:31,047
Otherwise, the lieutenant governor completes the term. Costs could rise if

21
00:01:31,047 --> 00:01:35,327
an additional special election is needed, or fall when replacement-candidate

22
00:01:35,327 --> 00:01:40,307
materials disappear. The analyst says the net effect is unknown, with

23
00:01:40,307 --> 00:01:43,527
possible costs or savings of millions per recall.

24
00:01:44,033 --> 00:01:48,613
A voter might want removal considered on its own, rather than as a shortcut

25
00:01:48,613 --> 00:01:54,153
for a candidate with limited support. Familiar vacancy procedures could make

26
00:01:54,153 --> 00:01:58,613
succession more predictable. That does not mean every replacement would be

27
00:01:58,613 --> 00:02:00,813
elected in a new majority vote.

28
00:02:01,367 --> 00:02:05,687
You might want to choose the replacement yourself, immediately. Another

29
00:02:05,687 --> 00:02:10,067
voter could object to giving appointments or automatic succession a larger

30
00:02:10,067 --> 00:02:14,927
role. They may prefer seeing the alternatives on the same ballot, and worry

31
00:02:14,927 --> 00:02:19,007
that a later election leaves them waiting. These are choices about

32
00:02:19,007 --> 00:02:22,147
accountability, not just administrative convenience.

33
00:02:23,033 --> 00:02:28,773
In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 35 percent said yes, 59

34
00:02:28,773 --> 00:02:34,833
percent no, and 5 percent were unsure. These are dated survey results, not

35
00:02:34,833 --> 00:02:35,953
votes already cast.

36
00:02:36,567 --> 00:02:41,287
Yes changes the replacement process; no keeps the replacement question on

37
00:02:41,287 --> 00:02:46,467
recall ballots. Visit our website for the fuller explanation, source links,

38
00:02:46,467 --> 00:02:50,867
and official voter guide. Compare the tradeoffs, then make your own informed

39
00:02:50,867 --> 00:02:51,707
choice.
